<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying a square root expression into simplest surd form</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Simplify \(\sqrt{{@number@}}\) to the simplest form. <i>(Note: Write <code>sqrt(3)</code> to represent \(\sqrt{3}\).)</i></p>
<p> Simplified form = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>To simplify \(\sqrt{{@number@}}\), apply the product rule for surds \(\displaystyle \sqrt{a \times b} = \sqrt{a} \times \sqrt{b}\) by finding the largest perfect-square factor of \({@number@}\).</p>

<p>Since \({@number@} = {@a@} \times {@b@}\), and \({@a@} = {@root_a@}^2\) is a perfect square:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@number@}} = \sqrt{{@a@} \times {@b@}} = \sqrt{{@a@}} \times \sqrt{{@b@}} = {@ta1@}\)</p>

<p><b>Key idea:</b></p>
<ul>
  <li>A surd is fully simplified when the number left inside the square root has no perfect-square factors (other than \(1\)).</li>
  <li>Always check for the <b>largest</b> perfect-square factor first. Otherwise, you may only simplify the surd part-way.</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \sqrt{{@number@}} = {@ta1@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
